#synthesis(title: [Pramipexole in ME/CFS: A Predicted Within-Patient Dissociation, Not a Treatment Claim])[
The body of evidence in this section assembles one argument whose load-bearing step is the framework's own ≤0.35 inference: pramipexole's established efficacy in its comorbid indication (RLS) is verified in idiopathic-RLS populations, and the same full D2/D3 agonism is predicted — not established — to worsen core ME/CFS symptoms (fatigue, effort allocation, motivation) at RLS doses in the D2-supersensitive subset (@spec:pramipexole-full-agonist-worsening-mecfs); the thesis is the within-patient coexistence of the two, a prediction at the framework's own certainty, not a settled mechanistic contradiction between two findings, and no cited dataset measured both halves in one patient (@cf:pramipexole-fatigue-analogue-conflict). The benefit side of the dissociation — any core-symptom improvement — is a prediction from the depression/anhedonia target-engagement data (@Ventorp2026PramipexoleAnhedonia, @Browning2025PAXDPramipexoleTRD), not an observed ME/CFS effect; the two halves (worsening on the D2-supersensitive core-symptom axis, possible benefit on the reward-salience axis) are predicted to coexist in the same patient. The benefit-side anchors sit above the 0.125–0.5 mg band where the dose is reported (PAX-D mean 2.3 mg at week 12) or at an unreported dose (the Ventorp dose is not stated in the cited source), while the harm claim is pinned to that band — a cross-dose extrapolation, and no dataset in any dose band shows both halves coexisting in one patient. What the evidence supports: the RLS dosing, monitoring, and taper framework is guideline-grade (AASM 2025; Allen 2014; Takahashi 2017 — the guideline de-ranking of dopamine agonists for chronic RLS is augmentation-risk-driven, not an efficacy dispute, @cf:rls-treatment-landscape), and the side-effect liabilities (ICD, DAWS, somnolence, orthostatic hypotension) are quantified in human studies. What remains speculative: every ME/CFS-specific mechanistic link — the supersensitivity worsening (certainty 0.35, the ME/CFS mechanistic cap), the low-dose aripiprazole attenuation (0.25, capped by Tadori's density-dependence in exactly the state it is meant to protect, @spec:pramipexole-lda-combo-attenuation), and the prolactin engagement readout (0.25, anchored at n = 5). The central open question is the falsifiable within-patient crossover: does pramipexole at RLS doses worsen core symptoms relative to an alpha-2-delta ligand in a supersensitive ME/CFS patient, and does the 0.25–1 mg aripiprazole arm cap side effects without psychiatric-range rescue? The secondary open questions — whether modafinil offsets sedation without eroding the RLS benefit (@oq:pramipexole-modafinil-combo) and whether elevated baseline prolactin enriches for response (@spec:pramipexole-prolactin-dopaminergic-tone-marker) — are capped at open-question and speculation status. The practical framing is therefore diagnostic, not therapeutic: bounded RLS dosing with core-symptom tracking is a test of the receptor-state model (@pwarn:pramipexole-mecfs-safety), and no ME/CFS efficacy claim is made or supported here.

*Consequence:* A patient on pramipexole for RLS should expect their core ME/CFS symptoms to be tracked as an outcome in their own right — they are the test of the supersensitivity model, not an expected benefit — and should understand that the drug's ME/CFS role is a stratified, ceiling-bounded trial protocol, not a treatment option.
] <syn:pramipexole-mecfs-model>
